llvm-3.7.1 local rule: the post-RA list scheduler takes a locked decrement
after every other available node
(no upstream state)

Adopted with lockdec-01 (docs/DECISIONS.md D97, round 598C), the tenth of the
line's nineteen in the glob's order (after lockdec-01, before ltopre-01). The
shared install under tools/cc is rebuilt with it in a round of its own; until
then the installed compiler does not hold it.

This patch CHANGES THE POST-RA LIST SCHEDULER'S PRIORITY ONLY.
LatencyPriorityQueue.cpp's latency_sort, after the isScheduleHigh test: a
node whose instruction is an x86 LOCK_DEC (8 to 64 bits) sorts below every
node that is not; between two such nodes, or two others, 3.7.1's order
(height, nodes solely blocked, NodeNum). Unchanged: the DAG, every latency
and edge, the region cuts, the anti-dependence breaker, every other locked
instruction (inc, xadd, cmpxchg, add, sub, and, or, xor), the pre-RA
scheduler. latency_sort serves the post-RA list scheduler and the VLIW
SelectionDAG scheduler (ScheduleDAGVLIW.cpp, not used by x86; its SUnits
have no MachineInstr and are left as they were). No other patch of the list
touches this file.

Question (rounds 569D, 587C, 590D, 592C): the base places free register
moves and extensions before a `lock dec` that 3.7.1 places after it: the
eleven 0x9f54b0 twins (`movzx eax, al; lock dec dword ptr [r14 + 0x30]`),
0x503710 (`mov r13, rbx` before the first drop), 0x7b2d50 (`mov rdi, r14`,
`mov rsi, r15; mov rdx, r13`), and, with lockdec-01, the seven 0xf1ae60
twins (the lea after the store).

Census (round 592C, c592/census.py; a region cut as PostRASchedulerList.cpp
:323-344 cuts it; a move FREE when no register, flags or memory path joins
it with the lock). The game region holds 110,443 locked instructions. Over
the drafts the base and the build disagree only at `lock dec`: the base has
25 free moves before a `lock dec` and 0 after, the build 0 before and 17
after (13 movzx, 4 mov); under this rule with lockdec-01 the build has 17
before and 0 after (round 598C, c598/census_drafts_rule.txt). Matched rows:
no free move next to a `lock dec`, either side. Of the base's 24 free moves
after a `lock dec dword` in the whole region, the nine read each read a
register made just before by a pending instruction, so the drop was the
only ready node.

Mechanism (llvm-3.7.1.src, read). The pass runs for btver2
(X86ScheduleBtVer2.td:24, PostRAScheduler = 1; PostRASchedulerList.cpp
:253-262), top-down by latency_sort (LatencyPriorityQueue.cpp:23-51: height,
then nodes solely blocked, then the lower NodeNum), with no active hazard
recognizer (no itinerary). A LOCK_DEC32m with no successor gets a live-out
edge to ExitSU of its latency less one (ScheduleDAGInstrs.cpp:853-858: 3,
WriteALULd's 4 on btver2) and a copy or extension none, so the drop is taken
first in any input order. A model alone cannot reverse it (round 598C, the
cut of 0x9f54b0's twin traced, c598/tw_m1.trace): at latency 1 or 0 the
live-out edge goes and the drop's height is 0, equal to the movzx's; the
tie falls to the nodes solely blocked (0 and 0) and then to the lower
NodeNum, the drop's (n0 against n1), because the instruction order that
reaches the pass, the IR's, has the atomicrmw before the zext. A height
cannot go below 0, so no latency of the model ranks the drop after a free
move of height 0; only the priority can.

Evidence (scratch c592/, c598/). The rule as a switch in a copy of the
installed compiler (c592/msx, c598/x; control, switch off: the whole game
module byte-identical, 0 symbols) and, with lockdec-01, built from the
pristine sources with every patch (c598/install, D97). Over the whole
module: alone 21 symbols, 0 matched rows lost, 13 drafts equal; with
lockdec-01 28 symbols, 0 rows lost, 20 drafts equal (FUN_00503710,
FUN_007b2d50, FUN_009f54b0 and its ten twins, the seven 0xf1ae60 twins),
none lower; FUN_0046f3d0 0.773 to 0.795. With lockdec-01, any width and 4
bytes or less give the same module byte for byte (alone, both 13 equal,
round 592C): the width is not told, and the rule takes lockdec-01's.

Candidates excluded, rows lost over the whole module (round 592C):
  the pass off (-post-RA-scheduler=false, -disable-post-ra): 3,417;
  -misched-postra: 2,908; -break-anti-dependencies none / all: 549 / 1,018;
  a locked instruction a region boundary: 129; 4 bytes or less: 87;
  latency_sort's NodeNum tie reversed: 3,697;
  no live-out edge for a lock: 4 lost, 1 equal; with its chain latency 0:
    11 lost, 1 equal;
  every locked instruction ranked last: 12 lost (ten dword `lock xadd`
    destructors of the 0xf33190 family, 0x13224a0 and 0x1322510, JsDevice::
    AllocateOnionMemory); only one with dead results: 1; only 4 bytes or
    less: 9;
  dead results and 4 bytes or less: 0 lost, 20 equal, but FUN_00cb5f90
    (0.959 to 0.949) and FUN_010f17f0 (0.214 to 0.179) move away: the base
    has `lock inc` first before a free lea or `mov r8d, imm`, so a `lock
    inc` keeps 3.7.1's rank and the kind is what the sites separate;
  a model alone (lockdec-01's header, m1 to m5): at most 1 equal.

Site against: 0x3ff7b3, in the unwritten FUN_003fd0a0. Its region (the block
from 0x3ff7a9 to the call at 0x3ff7c2) is `mov r15, rdx; mov r13, [rbp -
0x598]; lock dec dword ptr [r13]; mov rdi, r13; mov rbx, [rip + GOT];
call`. The move and the drop both wait on the load of r13 and the GOT load
on nothing, yet both come after the drop. The rule would place the move (and
the GOT load) before it; 3.7.1 would take the GOT load, free from the start
and with a live-out edge of its own, before the drop too. Neither gives the
region, so it does not separate the rule from 3.7.1; a written function
there would decide it.

Source forms excluded: none gives the order. The post-RA pass reverses the
IR order: the cut of 0x9f54b0's twin with the zext moved before the
atomicrmw in the IR (c592/twz.ll) has the base's order before the pass and
`lock dec; movzx` after it. 0x503710's and 0x7b2d50's moves are register
allocator copies with no source counterpart.

NO upstream state: no release from 3.3 to 4.0.0 ranks a locked instruction
in latency_sort or PostRASchedulerList. Sony authorship is not shown.

Trigger to revisit: a base region where a free move or extension ready with
a `lock dec` comes after it with a written function to show the input
order, or a matched row lost on a rebuild with the rule.

--- a/lib/CodeGen/LatencyPriorityQueue.cpp
+++ b/lib/CodeGen/LatencyPriorityQueue.cpp
@@ -14,12 +14,24 @@
 //===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
 
 #include "llvm/CodeGen/LatencyPriorityQueue.h"
+#include "llvm/CodeGen/MachineFunction.h"
 #include "llvm/Support/Debug.h"
 #include "llvm/Support/raw_ostream.h"
+#include "llvm/Target/TargetInstrInfo.h"
+#include "llvm/Target/TargetSubtargetInfo.h"
 using namespace llvm;
 
 #define DEBUG_TYPE "scheduler"
 
+// An x86 LOCK_DEC (8 to 64 bits): a locked decrement whose value is unused.
+static bool isLockedDec(const SUnit *SU) {
+  if (!SU->isInstr())
+    return false;
+  const MachineInstr *MI = SU->getInstr();
+  const TargetInstrInfo *TII = MI->getParent()->getParent()->getSubtarget().getInstrInfo();
+  return StringRef(TII->getName(MI->getOpcode())).startswith("LOCK_DEC");
+}
+
 bool latency_sort::operator()(const SUnit *LHS, const SUnit *RHS) const {
   // The isScheduleHigh flag allows nodes with wraparound dependencies that
   // cannot easily be modeled as edges with latencies to be scheduled as
@@ -29,6 +41,11 @@
   if (!LHS->isScheduleHigh && RHS->isScheduleHigh)
     return true;
 
+  // A locked decrement is taken after every other available node.
+  bool LHSDec = isLockedDec(LHS), RHSDec = isLockedDec(RHS);
+  if (LHSDec != RHSDec)
+    return LHSDec;
+
   unsigned LHSNum = LHS->NodeNum;
   unsigned RHSNum = RHS->NodeNum;
 
